    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6085",
      "problem": "Find the slope of each of the lines below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14166993/assets/e901704deb02efe957129512148c97e42b45b4fdd1d0e3af451562cca2ed1181.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of the vertical line is undefined. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(4,0)$ and $(4,1),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-0}{4-4}=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the horizontal line is zero. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,3)$ and $(1,3),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{3-3}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
